The current position has no immediate winning move for \(x\) (Player \(x\)) on any legal column, nor does \(o\) have an immediate winning threat that must be blocked. The existing pairs of pieces for both sides are either blocked on at least one end or lie too high to be completed by a single placement under gravity. Consequently the game is still in a building phase whose key strategic features are:

* the only two-move route to an open three (and therefore a genuine threat) runs through column 1;
* that route is double-edged: if \(o\) ever occupies cell \((4,1)\), \(x\) can answer at \((3,1)\) and instantly obtain two powerful threes (horizontal on row 3 and the diagonal through \((5,3)\)) that produce an unstoppable reloading threat sequence on column 0;
* the same cell also denies \(o\) its own fastest threat.

Because the hanging “snatch” already prevents \(o\) from safely using column 1, \(x\) need not spend a tempo there. The most profitable way to exploit the resulting tempo advantage is to develop the most multi-purpose empty column—column 4. A single stone at \((5,4)\):

* creates an immediate horizontal pair with the existing stone at \((5,3)\);
* prepares a pure vertical three that becomes a threat after only two further moves in the same column;
* simultaneously prepares the horizontal three on row 3 (cells \(2\)-\(4\)) that can later be turned into a second threat by two moves in column 5.

Whatever reply \(o\) chooses, \(x\) can force at least one threat by move 3 or 4 while \(o\) still possesses fewer than the three free moves required for a counter-threat of its own. Subsequent spoiling or extension moves then keep the initiative. All other first moves are inferior:

* column 1 itself enables \(o\)’s upper two-move threat and loses the race;
* columns 0/5/6 are edge files with fewer winning lines;
* column 2 or 3 places a stone too high to connect to the existing lower groups.

Hence the unique optimal opening move is column 4.

Final Answer: 4

